<h2 id="guide-heading">Guitar Discipline Machine practice guide</h2>
<details open><summary>How do I start a useful five-minute session?</summary><p>Choose E major, one neck region, and one note per pulse in Technique. In Timing, choose a tempo where you can fret each note cleanly and keep your picking hand relaxed. Press Start. Listen for the click and aim for an even attack. When the phrase feels steady twice in a row, try a small tempo increase.</p></details>
<details><summary>How do I learn the whole fretboard?</summary><p>Open Choose neck region. Learn the phrase in one region, then choose an overlapping pair and find where the notes connect. Try the same route descending before switching to Full neck. Groups of three and thirds give you different ways through familiar notes.</p></details>
<details><summary>How do chords and the keyboard help my ear?</summary><p>Choose C major in Theory. Set the four slots to I, IV, V, I and turn on the chord speaker. Play C, F, G and C as the backing changes. Then try landing on a different chord tone each time. The keyboard highlights the tones of the current chord while the harmony strip shows the chords you can choose.</p></details>
<details><summary>What does EXP measure?</summary><p>Today measures time with the transport running, including count-in. Use the EXP gear to set a daily target and choose your chart. Weekly bars show consistency. The scale chart ranks recorded exercise time across sessions. Open Practice journal for neck region, pattern, tempo and notes per bar. At 80 BPM, two notes per pulse ask for twice as many notes per minute as one. Listen for an even performance before raising the tempo.</p><p>Guest history stays in this browser. Clearing its site data removes that history.</p></details>
<details><summary>What informed the machine?</summary><p>Berklee PULSE recommends recording what you practiced and the tempo you used. Its technique lesson pairs a comfortable starting tempo with a goal. Those ideas informed the automatic session journal and adjustable tempo ramp. The EXP bars follow the Office for National Statistics guidance to measure bar length from zero.</p>
<ul><li><a href="https://pulse.berklee.edu/?id=4&amp;lesson=23">Berklee PULSE · Planning a practice routine</a></li><li><a href="https://pulse.berklee.edu/?id=4&amp;lesson=25">Berklee PULSE · Practicing technique</a></li><li><a href="https://service-manual.ons.gov.uk/data-visualisation/guidance/axes-and-gridlines">ONS · Chart axes and gridlines</a></li></ul></details></section>
